class="sticky navigation-sticky z-10 top-20 lg:top-0"><div
class="navigation-sticky__body"><div
class="lg:container"><div
class="lg:w-22/24 mx-auto"><div
class="relative py-4"><div
class="lg:flex lg:justify-between lg:items-center"><nav
class="navigation navigation--scrollsnap"><ul
class="navigation__list js-navigation-anchor"></ul></nav><div
class="lg:relative lg:top-0 lg:right-0 lg:ml-3 lg:p-0 lg:h-full lg:flex lg:items-center hidden">
<a
class="button w-full lg:w-auto" target="" href="https://www.ticketlandia.com/m/event/integrated-ticket-museums-area-archeological-en">Purchase the ticket</a></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="relative p-6 pb-0 lg:hidden block">
<a
class="button w-full lg:w-auto" target="" href="https://www.ticketlandia.com/m/event/integrated-ticket-museums-area-archeological-en">Purchase the ticket</a></div><section
class="js-section" data-section-id=" " data-section-title=""><div
class="container my-6 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-7 order-last lg:order-none"><h2 class="text-xl xl:text-3xl text-secondary mb-6 lg:mb-12">One of the most significant and best-preserved archaeological sites in Italy, recognised as a UNESCO World Heritage Site</h2><div
class="prose-ground text-primary mb-6 lg:mb-12"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The <em><span
class="post post-page current-item">Brixia Roman archeological area</span></em> offers one of the most significant and best-preserved routes in Italy. Recognised by UNESCO as a World Heritage Site in 2011, together with the San Salvatore – Santa Giulia monastic complex, the area has undergone an archaeological and architectural recovery enhancing this extraordinary area and making it fully accessible.</span></p></div></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-10 lg:col-span-15">
<img
decoding="async" width="1496" height="1008" src="data:image/png;base64," class="w-auto lazyload" alt=""   data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana.jpg 1496w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana-768x517.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana-1069x720.jpg 1069w" data-max-width="1496" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1496" data-eio-rheight="1008" /><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="1496" height="1008" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana.jpg" class="w-auto" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana.jpg 1496w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana-768x517.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Parco-archeologico-di-Brescia-romana-1069x720.jpg 1069w" sizes="(max-width: 1496px) 100vw, 1496px" data-eio="l" /></noscript></div></div></div></section><section
class="js-section" data-section-id=" " data-section-title=""><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16 xl:my-28"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-22"><div
class="prose-ground-highlighted text-primary-200"><p>We would like to inform our visitors that the Roman Theatre in Brescia is temporarily closed to the public whilst work begins to breathe new life into the city’s archaeological heart.</p></div></div></div></div></section><section
class="js-section" data-section-id="Archaeological-Area" data-section-title="Archaeological Area"><div
class="relative border-t border-gray-100"><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-span-6 xl:col-start-2"><h2 class="text-3xl xl:text-5xl text-primary">Archaeological Area</h2></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 xl:col-span-9 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-start-10 order-last lg:order-none"><div
class="prose-ground text-primary-200"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The city of Brescia still preserves the monumental remains of the ancient Roman Brixia, testifying to the city’s long history.</span></p></div></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-18 xl:col-start-19 lg:col-end-24"><div
class="lg:text-right">
<span
class="flex lg:justify-end items-center">
<a
class="text-lg has-hover-animation" target="_blank" href="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/parco-archeologico-di-brixia-romana-bw-foglio-a-strappo.pdf">Exhibition itinerary</a> <span
class="ml-2"><span
class="icon icon--arrow-right text-secondary"><svg
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" enable-background="new 0 0 24 24" height="24px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24px" fill="#000000"><rect
fill="none" height="24" width="24"/><path
d="M15,5l-1.41,1.41L18.17,11H2V13h16.17l-4.59,4.59L15,19l7-7L15,5z"/></svg></span></span>
</span></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-10 lg:col-span-14"><div
class="prose text-primary"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The </span><b>republican sanctuary (first century BCE)</b><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, the </span><b>Capitolium (73 CE)</b><span
style="font-weight: 400;"> with the remains of the imperial age and the nineteenth-century museum additions, the </span><b>Winged Victory</b><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, with part of the bronze collection and, lastly, the </span><b>Roman theatre (first – third century CE)</b><span
style="font-weight: 400;">.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">This area has been progressively unearthed and enhanced since 1823, when, thanks to a public subscription, the Ateneo di Scienze Lettere e Arti of Brescia began archaeological investigations based on the few elements that had surfaced on private property. The campaign led to extraordinary results, recognising the Capitoline Temple and many of its furnishings, the Roman theatre and the collection of large bronzes which included the Winged Victory. In 1830, the first of the city’s museums, the Museo Patrio, was inaugurated inside the restored Roman temple.</span></p></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-14 lg:col-start-10"><div
class="relative"><div
class="accordion accordion-702397 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-702397" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The republican sanctuary</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Underneath the Pallaveri House and the Capitoline Temple are the partially preserved structures of a sanctuary dating back to the early decades of the first century BCE. The remains were identified as early as 1823, studied between 1956 and 1961, and further investigated since the 1990s.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">It is a cultic complex consisting of four large rectangular halls placed side by side on a common podium, each with its own entrance and with a pronaos (portico with columns) for access, within a terrace overlooking the </span><i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">decumanus</span></i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, today’s Via dei Musei.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The interior of each hall presents surprisingly good and well-preserved fresco decorations. Painted white fluted columns, corresponding to the physical columns, amplify the space; mock polychrome marble slabs are supported by a frame, below which hangs a curtain revealing the underlying masonry; the overall decorative apparatus is adherent to Hellenistic models as interpreted by Roman craftsmen.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The discovery of vaulting elements has led to the hypothesis that these </span><i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">aulae</span></i><span
style="font-weight: 400;"> were covered by vaults set on a trabeation system or on architraves resting on the lateral rows of columns, as in the classic examples of the nymphaea of the Sillan period or the Pompeian Corinthian </span><i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">oeci</span></i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">This archaeological monument, unique in northern Italy, is attributable to high-level workers from central Italy, who were called to construct a building in Brescia to demonstrate the city’s adherence to the cultural model of Rome, in the years immediately following the granting of Latin citizenship (89 BCE).</span></p>
<picture><source  sizes='(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px' type="image/webp" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-scaled.jpg.webp 2560w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-768x511.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1623x1080.jpg.webp 1623w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1082x720.jpg.webp 1082w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1536x1022.jpg.webp 1536w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-2048x1363.jpg.webp 2048w"><img
decoding="async" width="2560" height="1703" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53935 lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""   data-eio="p" data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-scaled.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-768x511.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1623x1080.jpg 1623w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1082x720.jpg 1082w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1536x1022.jpg 1536w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-2048x1363.jpg 2048w" data-max-width="2560" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1920" data-eio-rheight="1277" /></picture><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="2560" height="1703" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53935" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-scaled.jpg" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-768x511.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1623x1080.jpg 1623w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1082x720.jpg 1082w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-1536x1022.jpg 1536w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-santuario-repubblicano-IV-cella-3368-2048x1363.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" data-eio="l" /></noscript></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-702398 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-702398" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The Capitolium</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The <em>Capitolium</em> was the main temple of every Roman city and was the very symbol of Roman culture.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Capitolium was dedicated to the cult of the “Capitoline Triad”, i.e. the main divinities of the Latin <em>pantheon</em>: Jupiter, Juno, and Minerva. The worshippers would gather in the temple foreground to attend the main ceremonies and sacrifices.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Today visitors can enter the <em>Capitolium</em> and see parts of its original decoration and furnishing. Inside, the original geometric coloured-marble pattern flooring (</span><i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">opus sectile</span></i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">) from the first century CE is still in place. In addition to the Botticino stone altars that were found here in the nineteenth century, fragments of cult statues and furnishings have also been arranged inside the cells. On the walls of the central hall is a display of the collection of epigraphs that assembled in 1830, when the Museo Patrio was inaugurated. </span></p>
<picture><source  sizes='(max-width: 1750px) 100vw, 1750px' type="image/webp" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3.jpg.webp 1750w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-768x595.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1394x1080.jpg.webp 1394w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-929x720.jpg.webp 929w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1536x1190.jpg.webp 1536w"><img
decoding="async" width="1750" height="1356" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53943 lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""   data-eio="p" data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3.jpg 1750w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-768x595.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1394x1080.jpg 1394w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-929x720.jpg 929w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1536x1190.jpg 1536w" data-max-width="1750" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1750" data-eio-rheight="1356" /></picture><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="1750" height="1356" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53943" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3.jpg" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3.jpg 1750w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-768x595.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1394x1080.jpg 1394w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-929x720.jpg 929w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-Archeologica-in-Capitolium-Capitolium-3-1536x1190.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1750px) 100vw, 1750px" data-eio="l" /></noscript></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-702399 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-702399" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The Winged Victory of Brescia</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The archaeological Park is home to the <a
href="https://www.bresciamusei.com/evento/palinsesto-vittoria-alata-brescia-2020/">Winged Victory</a>, a bronze masterpiece from the first century CE, which between 2019 and 2021 has been the subject of an exemplary interdisciplinary study and restoration carried out by the Opificio delle Pietre Dure in Florence.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The statue is now in its new collocation in the eastern cell of the <em>Capitolium,</em> in a museum setting designed by Spanish architect Juan Navarro Baldeweg, conceived to enhance the material and formal characteristics of the bronze statue, highlighted by the monumentality of the architecture and a luminous scenography.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Winged Victory has returned to where it was found: in 1826, during archaeological excavations carried out in the area by members of the Ateneo di Scienze, Lettere e Arti of Brescia, set inside two walls of the ancient <em>Capitolium</em>, this statue was discovered together with six imperial heads and hundreds of other bronze artifacts.</span></p><div
class="aspect-w-16 aspect-h-9"><iframe
loading="lazy" title="7 _ Il nuovo Capitolium e la Vittoria Alata © Febo Films" width="1778" height="1000"  frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen data-src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Xx_KPwMGy9M?start=15&amp;feature=oembed" class="lazyload"></iframe></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-702400 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-702400" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The Roman Theatre</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Next to the <em>Capitolium</em> is the Roman theatre, built and extended between the first and third centuries CE.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The </span><i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">cavea</span></i><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, the large space where spectators would sit, set on the Cidneo Hill slope welcomes the visitors evoking the atmosphere of ancient representations.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The layout of the building dates back to the Augustan age (end of the first century BCE – first century CE), and was subject to enlargements and improvements over the centuries, until the architectural decoration of the scene was renovated between the second and third centuries CE.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">This theatre was used until late antiquity (late fourth – early fifth century CE). Between the eleventh and twelfth centuries, the stage collapsed, probably due to an earthquake, and the building became an open-air quarry. </span><span
style="font-weight: 400;">In the twelfth century documents tell us this space was used as a place for public hearings, but its state of abandon and the crumbling of the hill led to its progressive and definitive burial.</span></p>
<picture><source  sizes='(max-width: 1452px) 100vw, 1452px' type="image/webp" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3.jpg.webp 1452w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-768x512.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-1080x720.jpg.webp 1080w"><img
decoding="async" width="1452" height="968" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53955 lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""   data-eio="p" data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3.jpg 1452w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-768x512.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-1080x720.jpg 1080w" data-max-width="1452" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1452" data-eio-rheight="968" /></picture><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="1452" height="968" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-53955" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3.jpg" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3.jpg 1452w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-768x512.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Area-archeologica-teatro-romano-maggior-risoluzioneteatro-3-1080x720.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 1452px) 100vw, 1452px" data-eio="l" /></noscript><p>&nbsp;</p></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-702401 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-702401" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The UNESCO Corridor</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p>The <b><em>UNESCO</em> <em>Corridor</em>, </b>inaugurated in June 2023<b>, is a monumental walk of approximately one kilometre through 2,500 years of history, which connects in a single pedestrian route, open free to the public, </b>the <b>Capitolium </b>area to the monumental complex of <b>Santa Giulia, </b>until now only accessible separately.</p><p><b>The <em>UNESCO</em><i> Corridor</i></b> thus <b>guarantees</b> all visitors, for the first time ever, <b>to remain immersed, visually and physically, in the historical monumental architecture </b>-from the Roman age to the early Middle Ages and the Renaissance-<b> without any interference between the present city and the aura of those ancient places</b>. The project, which indirectly generates the opening of two new public squares created in as many cloisters hitherto forbidden to visit except by visitors to the Santa Giulia Museum, was designed by architect Camillo Botticini for ARW Associates.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-16"><div
class="relative"><div
data-video-platform="youtube" data-video-id="6h3SCY0ZiG8" class="cursor-pointer js-video-trigger"><div
class="aspect-w-16 aspect-h-9 overflow-hidden">
